```c++
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

vector<int> multiplyVectors(vector<int> v1, vector<int> v2) {
    // Check for empty vectors
    if(v1.empty() || v2.empty()) {
        throw runtime_error("One or both of the input vectors are empty");
    }

    vector<int> result;

    // Multiply each element from v1 with every element from v2
    for(int i=0; i<v1.size(); i++) {
        for(int j=0; j<v2.size(); j++) {
            result.push_back(v1[i] * v2[j]);
        }
    }

    // Sort the result vector in ascending order
    sort(result.begin(), result.end());

    return result;
}
```

In this code, we first check if any of the vectors are empty. If they are, we throw an exception.

Next, we declare a vector `result` to store the results of the multiplication operations.

In the nested for loop, we multiply each element from the first vector with every element of the second vector, and push the result to the `result` vector.

Finally, we sort the `result` vector in ascending order using `std::sort`.

We call this function by passing two vectors and it will return a vector with results in sorted order. If any of the vectors is empty, it will throw a runtime error.

Note: This is a basic implementation. It does not handle cases such as overflow that could occur when multiplying large integers.

