Below is an implementation of the problem in Java. The algorithm is simple i.e it splits the given string into words and loops over each word to capitalize the first letter if it is not an article, conjunction, or a short preposition, except when it is the first word.

```java
public class TitleCaseFormatter {

    private static final HashSet<String> LOWERCASE_WORDS = new HashSet<String>();
    static {
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("a");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("an");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("and");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("as");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("at");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("by");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("for");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("in");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("of");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("on");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("or");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("the");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("to");
        LOWERCASE_WORDS.add("with");
    }

    public synchronized String titleCase(String title) {
        String[] words = title.split(" ");
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && LOWERCASE_WORDS.contains(words[i])){
                sb.append(words[i].toLowerCase());
            } else {
                sb.append(Character.toUpperCase(words[i].charAt(0))).append(words[i].substring(1).toLowerCase());
            }
            if (i < words.length - 1) {
                sb.append(' ');
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

The method `titleCase` of the `TitleCaseFormatter` class is thread-safe because it is `synchronized` which ensures that only one thread can enter this method at a time.

The Time complexity of the solution is O(n) where 'n' is the length of the string since we are iterating over the string once. The space complexity is O(1) because static space (for the HashSet of lowercase words) and dynamic space (StringBuilder and string manipulation inside the loop) is constant regardless of the size of the input string. The only exception to this is the `split` method which will create a new array proportional to the number of words, so in terms of number of words the space complexity can be considered as O(w).

